    ('h2', 'Budget ledger mechanics'),
    ('p', 'The monthly cap is enforced at the only place it cannot be bypassed: the transaction that records spend. Before any cloud call, the engine reads the month row set (BudgetEntry for YYYY-MM) in a transaction, sums running totals, and refuses the run if projected spend (estimate from token pricing in Setting prices.*) exceeds the cap; on success it writes the entry and the OK status in one commit. The estimate is conservative (max tokens for the tier), so the cap is never breached by a slow-finish run. Local runs write nothing - zero-cost tasks have zero ledger entries, which keeps the spend chart honest. The footer meter (Ch. 7) reads the same table, so what you see is what the engine enforces.'),
    ('h2', 'Redaction pipeline'),
    ('p', 'Redaction runs on every text payload before it can cross the network boundary, in rule priority order: REGEX rules first (fast, deterministic), then the local NER pass for PERSON, ORG, AMOUNT entities when enabled. MASK wraps the value in typed brackets ([EMAIL]), REPLACE substitutes a fixed token, SKIP drops the containing sentence entirely (used for the nastiest patterns, like card numbers, where even masked context is unwanted). The redacted payload, not the original, is what the cloud adapter ever receives; the adapter interface takes pre-redacted text by construction (R09\'s test asserts this at the adapter boundary). The Settings test pad runs any text through the pipeline and shows the diff, because a rule you cannot see working is a rule you cannot trust.'),
    ('h2', 'Prompt versioning and the evaluation harness'),
